<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  shared-mime-info package, installed to /usr/share/mime/packages/.

  WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL: on Linux the desktop entry's `MimeType=` only says
  which types ByteTable handles — it does not say what a file IS. That is
  shared-mime-info's job, and the mimetype ICON is looked up by the type the
  system decides on. Without this, a `.db` file on a stock system is usually
  `application/octet-stream`, so neither our association nor our icon applies to
  it, and the icons would look broken for the most common extension.

  `application/vnd.sqlite3` already exists in shared-mime-info; this ADDS the
  extensions it does not cover, and the magic match that identifies a real
  SQLite file whatever it is called.

  THE `*.db` GLOB IS DELIBERATELY WEAK (weight 40, below the default 50).
  `.db` is not ours — Thunderbird, Realm, Berkeley DB and others use it — so a
  package that claimed every `.db` on the user's system as SQLite would be
  rude and often wrong. At weight 40 any other definition wins, and the magic
  match above still identifies genuine SQLite files regardless of extension.

  NOT declared here: `.sql`. Distributions already map it, they just disagree on
  whether it is `application/sql` or `text/x-sql`, so the desktop entry claims
  BOTH rather than this file imposing one.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/vnd.sqlite3">
    <comment>SQLite database</comment>
    <magic priority="60">
      <match type="string" value="SQLite format 3" offset="0"/>
    </magic>
    <glob pattern="*.sqlite"/>
    <glob pattern="*.sqlite3"/>
    <glob pattern="*.db3"/>
    <glob pattern="*.db" weight="40"/>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
